Package: timeshift-tray
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         python3,
         python3-gi,
         gir1.2-glib-2.0,
         pkexec | polkitd,
         passwd,
         timeshift | timeshift-ssh,
         hicolor-icon-theme
Recommends: gnome-shell-ubuntu-extensions | gnome-shell-extension-appindicator
Description: Timeshift snapshot status in the system tray
 timeshift-tray puts a status icon in the desktop's tray showing when the last
 snapshot was taken, whether scheduled snapshots are actually running, and
 whether the snapshot location is reachable - and lets you take a snapshot on
 demand without opening the Timeshift window.
 .
 It shows live progress for any snapshot the machine is taking, not only ones
 started from the tray: a snapshot taken automatically before an apt operation
 appears with its phase and percentage while apt waits for it, which is
 otherwise invisible.
 .
 The applet runs as your own desktop user and never as root. It reads the
 read-only part of the Timeshift daemon's interface over
 /run/timeshift/daemon.sock, which cannot create, delete or restore anything.
 Taking a snapshot is a separate, authenticated action: it runs a wrapper with
 fixed arguments through pkexec, under a policy that authorises exactly that
 one command.
 .
 Reading the daemon's status requires membership of the "timeshift" system
 group. This package does not add anyone to it, because that membership also
 exposes the machine's disk layout and every snapshot path, and so is a
 decision for an administrator rather than for a postinst script. Until it is
 granted the applet says so plainly and offers to arrange it; the menu item
 runs "gpasswd -a" through pkexec, and the change takes effect at the next
 login. The menu also offers to take it away again, because a grant with no
 reverse is not one anybody should be asked to make.
 .
 A tray icon needs a host that implements the StatusNotifierItem protocol. KDE
 Plasma, Xfce, MATE and Cinnamon do so natively; GNOME Shell needs an
 appindicator extension, which is what the recommended package provides.
